{
  "schemaVersion": 2,
  "id": "chapter-01",
  "bookId": "sleepy-hollow",
  "number": 1,
  "title": "A Quiet Valley",
  "level": "a2",
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "p-01",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "In the quiet valley of "
        },
        {
          "text": "Sleepy Hollow",
          "vocabId": "sleepy-hollow"
        },
        {
          "text": ", the farms were rich and the people were slow to wake. "
        },
        {
          "text": "Mist",
          "vocabId": "mist"
        },
        {
          "text": " covered the fields in the morning, and the river moved slowly under the old wooden bridge. The village was small, but everyone knew everyone. Children played near the church, and old men talked for hours on the "
        },
        {
          "text": "porch",
          "vocabId": "porch"
        },
        {
          "text": " of the inn. It was a peaceful place, but it had a strange "
        },
        {
          "text": "reputation",
          "vocabId": "reputation"
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-02",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "People said the valley was "
        },
        {
          "text": "haunted",
          "vocabId": "haunted"
        },
        {
          "text": ". They told stories about a "
        },
        {
          "text": "headless",
          "vocabId": "headless"
        },
        {
          "text": " rider on a black horse. On dark nights, he rode through the "
        },
        {
          "text": "woods",
          "vocabId": "woods"
        },
        {
          "text": " looking for his lost head. Nobody had seen him, but everybody believed in him. Mothers used the story to make their children come home before sunset. Even the bravest farmers did not walk alone after "
        },
        {
          "text": "midnight",
          "vocabId": "midnight"
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-03",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "That autumn, a new teacher arrived in the village. His name was Ichabod Crane, and he was tall, thin, and always "
        },
        {
          "text": "curious",
          "vocabId": "curious"
        },
        {
          "text": ". He taught the children reading, writing, and "
        },
        {
          "text": "arithmetic",
          "vocabId": "arithmetic"
        },
        {
          "text": ", and he played the violin at village parties. He ate dinner at the farmers’ houses and listened to every story with wide eyes. Ichabod loved "
        },
        {
          "text": "ghost",
          "vocabId": "ghost"
        },
        {
          "text": " stories, but he loved good food even more."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-04",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "The teacher soon noticed Katrina Van Tassel, the beautiful daughter of a rich farmer. Many young men wanted to "
        },
        {
          "text": "marry",
          "vocabId": "marry"
        },
        {
          "text": " her, but the loudest and strongest was Brom Bones. Brom was a big, "
        },
        {
          "text": "confident",
          "vocabId": "confident"
        },
        {
          "text": " man with a red face and a quick "
        },
        {
          "text": "temper",
          "vocabId": "temper"
        },
        {
          "text": ". He liked to play tricks on people, and he did not like the thin new teacher. When Ichabod looked at Katrina, Brom’s eyes grew dark."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-05",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "One evening, Ichabod was invited to a party at the Van Tassel farm. He put on his best clothes and borrowed a horse from a "
        },
        {
          "text": "neighbor",
          "vocabId": "neighbor"
        },
        {
          "text": ". The horse was old, slow, and not very "
        },
        {
          "text": "handsome",
          "vocabId": "handsome"
        },
        {
          "text": ", but Ichabod felt like a king. At the party, he danced with Katrina and told stories until the fire burned low. The other guests laughed and "
        },
        {
          "text": "applauded",
          "vocabId": "applauded"
        },
        {
          "text": ", but Brom watched from a corner in "
        },
        {
          "text": "silence",
          "vocabId": "silence"
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-06",
      "segments": [
        {
          "text": "Late at night, Ichabod said goodbye to Katrina and rode home alone. The road was dark, and the wind made the trees "
        },
        {
          "text": "whisper",
          "vocabId": "whisper"
        },
        {
          "text": ". He remembered the old stories and tried to "
        },
        {
          "text": "hum",
          "vocabId": "hum"
        },
        {
          "text": " a song, but his voice was weak. Then he heard a sound behind him. It was slow and heavy, like a horse walking in the dark."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
